A||||8||burdell1||WATER LEAKAGE!
Z||000000||burdell1||05-22-99||08:50 AM||||I was driving my '85 GT last night and all of a sudden the temp gauge when rapidly into the red. The car would still run (I shut if off immediately, though) and coolant was heavily leaking out of the driver's side in the rear of the car.  I know it could be a lot of things, but does anyone have any ideas? I put around a gallon and a 1/2 more coolant in today and was able to drive it home. I don't have much time to deal with it at the moment so I'll have to just leave it for the time being. 
Z||000001||Phil||05-23-99||06:15 AM||||That's right where your hoses are. 
Z||000002||burdell1||05-23-99||10:16 AM||||I did some investigating and found that the rubber tube that connects the coolant tubes on the driver's side (towards the rear tire) is leaking.  is this very easy to fix?  it looks easy, but I thought maybe there was something more to it.  Also, I need to buy some ramps for my car to fix it, but was wondering how safe they are. i
Z||000003||theogre||05-23-99||01:11 PM||||get ramps and extensions. the ramps by themselves are very tricky to get onto, and it's very easy to over shoot the tops. It's even worse with stick cars. There are plastic extension block that work really nice. Some people, myself included, use a 3 foot peice of 2X6 but you have to be very careful how you place the board. If you aren't the board can move on you, and that can produce some very not pretty results.<p>As for the safety of ramps.... Fiero is not even close to the rated working load of any of the available sets I've seen. If you are worried at all, put a big block of wood under the ramp. then it can only drop an inch or to if there ever is a problem.<p>Don't use ramps on blacktop. they will sink into the surface. at least nearly all the stamped steel ones will. 
Z||000004||Phil||05-23-99||06:07 PM||||Now that the car is up in the air just loosen the clamps pull off the old hose put on a new  one and tighten the clamps. Zip zip zip nothing to it Unless of course--------
Z||000005||Bruce||06-02-99||07:46 PM||||I have a coolant leak which seems to be from around the area of the oil filter. Is this just a loose hose, over-filling the radiator, or something else?<br>thanks
Z||000006||theogre||06-03-99||12:13 PM||||There is a coolant passage in the intake manifold. the gasket could be blown. I don't think there are any soft plugs near that area. could also be the head gasket.<p>if it's comming down the "front" (pully end) of the motor the cover that plugs the water jacket opening may be leaking. (If you have the serpent belt, the tesioner forms this cover as well.)
Z||000007||Bruce||06-03-99||12:32 PM||||may be an oil leak, on closer inspection, since the fluid is much clearer than coolant. it drips to the inside of the right rear wheel. the temp. gauge doesn't indicate any rapid increase, so i'm thinking it's not a cooling system problem. any ideas that I can give to the mechanic during tomorrow's visit?<br>thanks!!
Z||000008||Bruce||06-04-99||12:41 PM||||There's a coolant leak in the pipe near the air conditioning unit- a minor welding job, but a lot of effort to get there. But, not as bad as it I thought.
